": "attempt", "is_correct": true, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-18T15:18:23Z", "username": "Abhishek Vats", "text": "Mast Ques ‎", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-18T15:18:33Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "Vats: 45, Srikanth: 10, Srijan: 10, KP: 15", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-18T15:18:36Z", "username": "Srijan Naayak", "text": "So you were an impact player today.", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-18T15:19:17Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "next", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-18T15:19:34Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "6.\tOn the evening of April 18, 1775, X set out with William Dawes and Samuel Prescott on an urgent mission to warn their friends in the town of Y about an approaching threat. Before they could reach their destination, they were intercepted by authorities. X was sent back to where he came from, but Dawes and Prescott managed to escape and reach Y and warned their colleagues. Z wrote a poem in 1860 taking significant liberties with the truth, claiming that X arrived in Y at 2 am and alerted its people.  This poem changed the popular memory of what happened on that April night, resulting in X being venerated for his role while Dawes and Prescott are mostly forgotten. The poem begins thus: “Listen, my children, and you shall hear / Of the midnight ____ of X.” Even though X himself didn’t make it to Y on that night in reality, the trio’s ride did change the course of history. ID X (5 points), Y (10 points), and Z (5 points). ‎<attached: 00120628-PHOTO-2026-04-18-08-19-34.jpg>", "role": "elaboration", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-04-18T15:19:39Z", "username": "Akshay", "tex